WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.134 --> 00:00:17.416
For the next three weeks, Anne and Priscilla continued to feel like strangers in a strange land.

2
00:00:17.417 --> 00:00:25.716
But as they attended classes and social events, and became familiar with the professors and other students, Anne and Priscilla felt better.

3
00:00:25.717 --> 00:00:30.567
The first-year students, instead of being a collection of unrelated individuals,

4
00:00:30.568 --> 00:00:38.592
became close acquaintances who all attended class events and shared a class song and class spirit.

5
00:00:38.593 --> 00:00:47.974
Gilbert Blythe was elected class president. He was also invited to join a fraternity, which was a rare honor for a first-year student.

6
00:00:47.975 --> 00:00:50.441
As an initiation to the fraternity,

7
00:00:50.442 --> 00:00:58.172
Gilbert had to walk down the main streets of Halifax for a whole day wearing a bonnet and a colorful kitchen apron.

8
00:00:58.173 --> 00:01:02.810
He did this cheerfully, shaking ladies' hands as they passed.

9
00:01:02.811 --> 00:01:08.243
Anne and Priscilla found themselves very involved in the social life of Redmond College.

10
00:01:08.244 --> 00:01:12.225
Philippa Gordon came from a rich and well-known family.

11
00:01:12.226 --> 00:01:17.715
This, along with her beauty and charm, got her invited to many parties and events.

12
00:01:17.716 --> 00:01:21.899
And wherever she went, Anne and Priscilla went too.

13
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:29.754
It was a mystery to Anne how Phil found time to study because she was always going out, especially with young men.

14
00:01:29.755 --> 00:01:35.331
Nine-tenths of the male students in first year were rivals for Phil's smiles.

15
00:01:35.332 --> 00:01:44.390
"Your two special hometown 'friends', Alec and Alonzo, don't seem to have any serious rivals yet," remarked Anne teasingly.

16
00:01:44.391 --> 00:01:50.170
"Not one," agreed Philippa. "Of course, I can't get the boy I like best.

17
00:01:50.171 --> 00:01:56.034
Gilbert Blythe won't take any notice of me, except to look at me as if I'm a little kitten he'd like to pat.

18
00:01:56.035 --> 00:02:01.438
I know the reason, Anne. It's clear that Gilbert likes you.

19
00:02:01.439 --> 00:02:05.466
I really ought to hate you, but instead I adore you!

20
00:02:05.467 --> 00:02:09.049
Around you, I wish I was wiser and stronger.

21
00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:12.653
Anne, please tell me over again that you like me a little bit!"

22
00:02:12.654 --> 00:02:21.230
"I like you a lot! And I think you're a dear, sweet, adorable little . . . kitten." Anne laughed.

23
00:02:21.231 --> 00:02:26.320
"But I don't understand how you ever have time to study!"

24
00:02:26.321 --> 00:02:34.673
Somehow Phil must have found time to study because she was the top student in every class except English, where Anne came first by far.

25
00:02:34.674 --> 00:02:40.446
Anne found her studies easy, leaving her time for a social life that she thoroughly enjoyed.

26
00:02:40.447 --> 00:02:47.504
But never for a moment did she forget Avonlea and her family and friends there.

27
00:02:47.505 --> 00:02:52.011
For Anne, the happiest time of each week was when she read letters from home.

28
00:02:52.012 --> 00:03:01.682
The first batch had contained letters from Ruby Gillis, Diana, Marilla, Mrs. Lynde, and Jane Andrews, who had taken over as the new schoolteacher.

29
00:03:01.683 --> 00:03:06.323
Jane's letter was neatly written, without an interesting sentence in it.

30
00:03:06.324 --> 00:03:11.661
She didn't answer any of the questions that Anne had asked in her letter to Jane.

31
00:03:11.662 --> 00:03:18.330
But Jane did tell Anne how many yards of lace she had crocheted and how she felt when her head ached.

32
00:03:18.331 --> 00:03:24.358
Ruby Gillis wrote about how much she was missing Anne and asked what the Redmond boys were like.

33
00:03:24.359 --> 00:03:30.838
Ruby's letter was silly and harmless, and Anne would have laughed except for one part.

34
00:03:30.839 --> 00:03:35.737
"Gilbert seems to be enjoying Redmond, judging from his letters," wrote Ruby.

35
00:03:35.738 --> 00:03:38.748
"I don't think Charlie likes it so much."

36
00:03:38.749 --> 00:03:48.532
So Gilbert was writing to Ruby! "Gilbert can write to anybody he wants to, of course," thought Anne, "but . . ."

37
00:03:48.533 --> 00:03:52.119
Anne was completely confused by her feelings.

38
00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:59.526
What Anne didn't know was that Ruby had written to Gilbert first and that he had simply answered to be polite.

39
00:03:59.527 --> 00:04:04.034
Marilla's letter was rather prim, with no gossip or emotion.

40
00:04:04.035 --> 00:04:09.173
Yet somehow it conveyed the wholesome, simple life at Green Gables.

41
00:04:09.174 --> 00:04:12.974
Mrs. Lynde's letter was full of church news.

42
00:04:12.975 --> 00:04:20.018
"I wonder," said Anne as she folded up her letters, "what Mrs. Lynde would think of Philippa."

43
00:04:20.019 --> 00:04:28.227
One fine winter day, Gilbert, Anne, Priscilla, Charlie, and Phil met to go for a walk through Halifax.

44
00:04:28.228 --> 00:04:31.925
"Let's go down Spofford Avenue," suggested Gilbert.

45
00:04:31.926 --> 00:04:35.350
"We can see all the beautiful houses where the rich people live.

46
00:04:35.351 --> 00:04:39.091
Nobody can live there unless he or she is a millionaire."

47
00:04:39.092 --> 00:04:45.893
"Oh, yes!" said Phil. "There's a perfectly gorgeous little place I want to show you, Anne.

48
00:04:45.894 --> 00:04:53.242
It wasn't built by a millionaire. I swear it must have grown out of the ground while Spofford Avenue was still a country road.

49
00:04:53.243 --> 00:04:58.641
I don't like the other houses there; they're too new and showy."

50
00:04:58.642 --> 00:05:01.997
They saw the small house as they walked up the hill.

51
00:05:01.998 --> 00:05:05.481
It was white, with pine trees on both sides.

52
00:05:05.482 --> 00:05:10.309
The whole place looked like it had been transplanted from some remote country village.

53
00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:15.224
"It's the sweetest place I've ever seen," said Anne, delighted.

54
00:05:15.225 --> 00:05:20.526
"It's even quainter than Miss Lavender's house."

55
00:05:20.527 --> 00:05:30.574
"Look at the name, Anne," said Phil, "in white letters around the archway over the gate: 'Patty's Place.' Isn't that gorgeous?"

56
00:05:30.575 --> 00:05:34.564
"Have you any idea who Patty is?" asked Priscilla.

57
00:05:34.565 --> 00:05:43.340
"Patty Spofford is the name of the old lady who owns it, I've discovered," said Phil. "She lives there with her niece."

58
00:05:43.341 --> 00:05:47.950
"I'm going to dream about Patty's Place tonight," said Anne.

59
00:05:47.951 --> 00:05:53.202
"I wonder if, by any chance, we'll ever see the inside of it."

60
00:05:53.203 --> 00:05:56.253
"It isn't likely," said Priscilla.

61
00:05:56.254 --> 00:06:03.772
Anne smiled mysteriously. "No, it isn't likely, but I believe it will happen.

62
00:06:03.773 --> 00:06:15.388
I have a strange feeling that Patty's Place and I are going to become better acquainted."
